# The corpus' first ASYMPTOTIC Java-vs-OCaml gap: unbounded in a parameter,
# not a constant factor. Every other gap on record (worst: lean-rbmap-mono at
# 34x) is bounded and traceable to data representation -- two allocations per
# `Rt.Data` node, boxed `Long`, `Rt.java`'s O(n)-copy arrays. This one is in the
# GENERATOR's treatment of `const` and grows without bound in N.
#
# Arend-authored (ExampleConstBlowup.constBlowupProgram): prog.ast is
# `ExamplePrint:constBlowupSexpr`, regenerated by test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh.
# prog.attr is a copy of runtime/peano/empty.attr -- the program realizes no
# axioms. N lives in one place, `ExampleConstBlowup.blowupLevels`, currently 30.
#
# THE MECHANISM. ToJava.ard:267 compiles `const k` to a call of a zero-argument
# `public static Object` method whose body is the constant's entire λ□ body
# (compileDecl, ToJava.ard:348-365), with no memoization anywhere -- so a
# constant referenced twice is EVALUATED twice. Peregrine's OCaml backend emits
# `(module ($def__foo <expr>) ...)`, a module binding evaluated once at load,
# and its C backend emits one `body()` with a local per constant. Both are
# linear; ours is exponential on a constant graph that is a DAG.
#
# Verified in work/const-blowup/ocaml/bench.mlf: 62 = 2N+2 distinct bindings,
# each `(apply $def__pick $def__aK $def__bK)` referencing VARIABLES, not a
# duplicated body. And in work/const-blowup/c/prog.c: one `body()` with
# registers `$da29_*`/`$db29_*` computed once and used twice.
#
# This is NOT a `letIn` problem -- ToJava.ard:279-283 compiles `letIn` to a real
# Java local, evaluated once. It is specifically `const`.
#
# THE PROGRAM. Two interleaved chains (see the module header for why the obvious
# single chain does not work as a corpus program):
#
#   a0 = true                b0 = false
#   a(k+1) = pick a(k) b(k)  b(k+1) = pick b(k) a(k)      main = a N
#
# `pick x y` matches on `y` and returns `x`, so every a(k) is `true` and the
# answer is 1 -- but both references are load-bearing, and Java re-runs each
# subtree. `pick` matching its SECOND argument is what stops the λ□ middle end
# or HotSpot from dropping the call as dead.
#
# MEASUREMENTS (this machine, best of 3, the harness's own JAVA_RUN_FLAGS):
#
#   N     java_run   ocaml_run
#   24      3.09 s     0.016 s
#   28      5.31 s     0.033 s
#   30      8.0 s      < 0.1 s     <- committed; the harness's own numbers
#
# Read java_run, not the ratio, as the evidence of growth: ocaml_run here is
# process startup (16-33 ms measured by hand) and is flat in N, so the ratio is
# a noise quotient -- its exact value (190x-550x across those rows, >160x at the
# committed N however ocaml_run's sub-0.1 s reading is rounded) is not
# meaningful to two digits, though it clears 100x by any reading.
#
# HOTSPOT MASKS PART OF THIS, which is worth knowing and cost the first version
# of this program. `c___aK()` is a nullary pure static call, so duplicated calls
# are exactly what the inliner plus CSE collapse. On the single-chain shape
# (`d(k+1) = pick d(k) d(k)`, two IDENTICAL calls) the cost went flat above
# N=26 -- 0.10/0.63/4.21/4.33/4.96 s at N=20/24/26/28/30 -- so it stopped
# responding to N before reaching a useful running time. That the generated code
# is nonetheless exponential was confirmed by turning the optimizations off:
#
#   single chain, N=24:  default 0.77 s   -XX:-Inline  0.91 s   -Xint  7.45 s
#   single chain, N=30:  default 4.46 s   -XX:-Inline 40.51 s   -Xint  > 5 min
#
# i.e. 2.07x per +1 in N interpreted, ~1.9x per +1 with inlining off, and it is
# INLINING specifically -- not the JIT as such -- that masks it. Interleaving two
# chains makes each body's two references calls to DIFFERENT methods, which CSE
# cannot merge directly, and the cost keeps growing where the single chain's had
# stopped. Note also that java_run at N=30 runs at ~300% CPU: a real share of it
# is HotSpot compiling the generated methods on background threads. That does not
# weaken the finding -- it is still cost this generated code imposes and the
# other two backends do not -- but it is part of the 8 s.
#
# THE COST IS TIME, NOT MEMORY, by construction: the result is a nullary
# constructor, so every allocation dies immediately. The obvious `node a(k) b(k)`
# tree would hold 2^N LIVE nodes and turn this into an OutOfMemoryError instead
# of a timing. Confirmed: the program prints 1 in a 16 MB heap at BOTH N=24 and
# N=30 (`-Xmx16m`), i.e. the live set is flat in N. Unconstrained max RSS does
# grow (250 MB at N=24, 848 MB at N=30), but that is G1 sizing itself against
# the allocation rate plus a larger JIT code cache, not a growing live set --
# which is why the -Xmx figure is the one that answers the question.
#
# Cost is in the GENERATED CODE, not the generator: java_gen 29 s and
# java_build 1.6 s are in the same range as the other handwritten programs
# (peano 31 s, matmul 38 s), because the generated Java is only 2N+3 tiny
# methods. N is the one number to change; 8 s leaves a wide margin under
# run.py's 300 s default timeout.
expected=1
backends=java ocaml c
runtime=peano
xfail=
note=Bool.true; a_k/b_k = pick of the previous pair, so Java re-evaluates constants 2^N times where OCaml/C bind them once
